{"text":"Recent work supports learning directional item relationships separately from broad similarity. [Revisiting Graph Projections (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.09209) constructs a directed, weighted item graph from interaction histories, combining co-purchase strength with temporal ordering. It offers a lightweight alternative to neural similarity retrieval without pretrained weights. [SComGNN (AAAI 2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.02130) models both relevance and dissimilarity through low-pass and mid-pass graph filters: complements can be related while differing substantially in function or appearance.

Compatibility remains a separate uncertainty. [ECCR (2024)](https://genai-personalization.github.io/assets/papers/GenAIRecP2024/Li.pdf) emphasizes that co-purchase does not establish complementarity, and a plausible product-type pairing does not establish item-level compatibility. Its method fine-tunes an LLM, so it does not meet the scratch-only constraint; its distinction between behavioral association and actual compatibility remains useful. Interaction sequences alone cannot reliably label substitutes versus complements.

For this benchmark, a plausible adaptation—not a published result—is a SASRec residual combining smoothed directed transition scores with separate source/target embeddings and a small title/description encoder trained from scratch. Token or character features could capture explicit model identifiers and compatibility phrases. Keep text similarity and transition evidence as separate signals; treat missing compatibility evidence as unknown. Train solely on training histories, preserve category routing, and select the residual strength using macro validation HR@10. These papers motivate the design but do not establish gains under the pinned RecHarness evaluation."}
